commissioner justin jones he is an avid  bike rider represents the southwest  valley  one day after what took place yesterday  he wants all of us to be better and  safer on the road  we have kate houston live in the  newsroom with a conversation she had  with him earlier today  kate yes commissioner jones has been  cycling for 15  years during that time he's worked  closely with local organizations to  promote safe riding  here in southern nevada jones says while  there are laws to protect cyclists  more needs to be done he says oftentimes  major changes aren't made until  after a tragedy happens much like the  one that occurred yesterday  jones wants motorists to understand that  cyclists have the right to be in the  lane of travel  and they should be given three feet of  room commissioner jones tells me the  cycling community in southern nevada is  very close  and what happened yesterday has had a  profound impact  yesterday was a tough day a lot of my  friends  who are cyclists it was just it's it's a  rough time  um you know they're one of my good  friends  uh he said he's he's likely done with  with uh road biking because of this  he unfortunately got hit by a motorist a  year or so ago  and uh you know we need to be able to to  to not  throw in the towel right we need to be  able to be in a situation where you can  feel safe  getting out there on on bikes because  it's it's what a lot of us love  right now commissioner jones is working  with the southern nevada bicycle  coalition  and ghost bikes to encourage safe  cycling  he wants southern nevadans to use this  as an opportunity to remind themselves  of the laws that are in place to protect  both drivers  and cyclists he tells me his heart is  with the families of those that died  and with those that were there to  witness what happened he's worried that  the trauma will be long  lasting
 

